    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Room to Move\"\nsubtitle: \"Question 3 — Puma connectivity\"\n---\n\nThe puma question is the opposite of the bobcat one. There are too few puma\nrecords to model where the animals are, and the precise locations that do exist\nare sensitive enough that publishing them would do harm. So this section does not\nmap where pumas were seen. It maps where the landscape would let one move — built\nfrom the shape of the land, not from sightings.\n\n## Built from resistance, not sightings\n\nEvery layer in this section rests on a resistance surface: a map of how costly\neach 1 km cell is for a puma to cross, high where the land is developed, steep, or\nbusy with roads, low where it is protected open ground. The surface is assembled\nfrom land cover, terrain, human modification, and traffic — not from where anyone\nrecorded a puma.\n\nThat has one consequence worth stating up front, because it is the mirror image of\nthe bobcat result. The bobcat occupancy model had to *correct* for recording\neffort, because it was built from sightings. The puma corridors carry no effort\nterm at all — there are no sightings in them to be biased. Where the puma corridors\nagree with a density surface, they agree for a structural reason, not because both\nwere shaped by the same observers. This is the clean half of Question 5.\n\n## Why you see a band, not a line\n\nThe core result is a corridor between the two largest habitat blocks in the\nregion: the Santa Cruz Mountains and the southern Diablo Range, linked across the\nsouth Bay through the Coyote Valley pinch where US-101 cuts the gap. The\nleast-cost path between them runs 37.2 km and passes within 1.6 km of the verified\nCoyote Valley crossing.\n\nBut the maps below lead with a **band**, not that line, and the reason is a real\nlimit, not a stylistic choice. A least-cost path is a single-cell-wide thread\nthrough a 1 km grid. It looks precise, and that precision is false: move the input\nweights a little and the thread shifts by a kilometre or more. The honest product\nis the **swath** — the zone of low-cost cells around the path — shown in two tiers:\n\n- a **core** band (the lowest-cost 2% of corridor cells, 409 km²) — high-confidence corridor,\n- a **context** band (the lowest-cost 5%, 1,021 km²) — the permeable flanks around it.\n\nThe swath says what the analysis can actually support: *the corridor runs through\nhere, in this zone*, not *the corridor is this exact line*. The precise pinch at\nCoyote Valley is located separately, by road geometry in the crossing analysis\nbelow — not by the swath, whose 1 km grain is too coarse to resolve a\nsub-kilometre constriction.\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n::: {#fig-puma-swath .cell-output-display}\n\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"leaflet html-widget html-fill-item\" id=\"htmlwidget-211b1ba64c12f4322296\" style=\"width:100%;height:464px;\"></div>\n<script type=\"application/json\" data-for=\"htmlwidget-211b1ba64c12f4322296\">{\"x\":{\"options\":{\"minZoom\":7,\"crs\":{\"crsClass\":\"L.CRS.EPSG3857\",\"code\":null,\"proj4def\":null,\"projectedBounds\":null,\"options\":{}}},\"calls\":[{\"method\":\"addTiles\",\"args\":[\"https://server.arcgisonline.com/ArcGIS/rest/services/Canvas/World_Light_Gray_Base/MapServer/tile/{z}/{y}/{x}\",null,null,{\"minZoom\":0,\"maxZoom\":18,\"tileSize\":256,\"subdomains\":\"abc\",\"errorTileUrl\":\"\",\"tms\":false,\"noWrap\":false,\"zoomOffset\":0,\"zoomReverse\":false,\"opacity\":1,\"zIndex\":1,\"detectRetina\":false,\"attribution\":\"Tiles &copy; Esri\"}]},{\"method\":\"addTiles\",\"args\":[\"https://server.arcgisonline.com/ArcGIS/rest/services/Canvas/World_Light_Gray_Reference/MapServer/tile/{z}/{y}/{x}\",null,null,{\"minZoom\":0,\"maxZoom\":18,\"tileSize\":256,\"subdomains\":\"abc\",\"errorTileUrl\":\"\",\"tms\":false,\"noWrap\":false,\"zoomOffset\":0,\"zoomReverse\":false,\"opacity\":1,\"zIndex\":1,\"detectRetina\":false}]},{\"method\":\"addPolygons\",\"args\":[[[[{\"lng\":[-122.1634054281192,-122.1636625