22.0534483127046,-122.0421691288526,-122.0426552265956,-122.0652189023426,-122.0654647220366,-122.0880308477629,-122.0882794105746,-122.0995637271336,-122.0990639764466],\"lat\":[37.21522111334323,37.21561728740366,37.20662335645498,37.20701520364951,37.19802120981807,37.19821550546635,37.21620352169106,37.21581376908146,37.22480770629085,37.22441362651155,37.23340750097339,37.23320883225639,37.21522111334323]}],[{\"lng\":[-121.7150489417275,-121.7154572122484,-121.7041737483862,-121.7037681621148,-121.7150489417275],\"lat\":[37.20338459251677,37.22137382017994,37.22153605015568,37.2035467844758,37.20338459251677]}],[{\"lng\":[-121.7490991246507,-121.7493073109514,-121.7380239938503,-121.7378171497815,-121.7490991246507],\"lat\":[37.2118861597107,37.22088070740889,37.22104614879969,37.21205158171303,37.2118861597107]}],[{\"lng\":[-121.7605905784935,-121.7608001568868,-121.7495155468111,-121.7493073109514,-121.7605905784935],\"lat\":[37.22071419556022,37.22970870724102,37.22987523861213,37.22088070740889,37.22071419556022]}],[{\"lng\":[-121.7380239938503,-121.7384378297244,-121.7497238322474,-121.7499321672782,-121.7386448215649,-121.7388518626979,-121.7275631241647,-121.7273574265592,-121.7160699825808,-121.7158656771733,-121.7271517779265,-121.7269461782491,-121.7156614204016,-121.7154572122484,-121.7380239938503],\"lat\":[37.22104614879969,37.23903523373271,37.23886975353906,37.24786425240833,37.24802975201637,37.25702425446977,37.25718870253433,37.24819418078339,37.24835753870249,37.23936298196643,37.23919964321079,37.23020508959779,37.23036840919847,37.22137382017994,37.22104614879969]}],[{\"lng\":[-121.750140551921,-121.7499321672782,-121.7612194633793,-121.7614291915143,-121.750140551921],\"lat\":[37.25685873543867,37.24786425240833,37.24769768196618,37.25669214544797,37.25685873543867]}]]],null,null,{\"interactive\":true,\"className\":\"\",\"stroke\":true,\"color\":\"#4a2409\",\"weight\":0.6,\"opacity\":0.8,\"fill\":true,\"fillColor\":\"#7d3f16\",\"fillOpacity\":0.7,\"smoothFactor\":1,\"noClip\":false},null,null,\"Core band — lowest-cost 2% (409 km²), high-confidence corridor\",{\"interactive\":false,\"permanent\":false,\"direction\":\"auto\",\"opacity\":1,\"offset\":[0,0],\"textsize\":\"10px\",\"textOnly\":false,\"className\":\"\",\"sticky\":true},null]},{\"method\":\"addControl\",\"args\":[\"<div style='background:#fff;padding:8px 10px;border-radius:4px;\\n                   box-shadow:0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,0.2);font-size:12px;\\n                   line-height:1.5;text-align:left;max-width:200px;'>\\n         <div style='font-weight:600;margin-bottom:5px;'>Puma corridor swath<\\/div>\\n         <div style='display:flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;'>\\n           <span style='width:15px;height:13px;background:#7d3f16;border:1px solid #4a2409;\\n                        display:inline-block;flex:none;'><\\/span>Core (409 km²)<\\/div>\\n         <div style='display:flex;align-items:center;gap:7px;'>\\n           <span style='width:15px;height:13px;background:#d8a25e;border:1px solid #b98545;\\n                        display:inline-block;flex:none;'><\\/span>Context (1,021 km²)<\\/div>\\n         <div style='margin-top:5px;color:#5a6070;font-size:11px;'>\\n           Corridor runs through this zone, not a single line. No puma locations shown.<\\/div>\\n       <\\/div>\",\"bottomright\",null,\"info legend\"]}],\"fitBounds\":[36.85065,-123.53354,38.86399,-121.2083,[]],\"limits\":{\"lat\":[36.99340903342093,37.30575242307333],\"lng\":[-122.2226289157264,-121.6354986097792]}},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":[]}</script>\n```\n\n\nThe Santa Cruz Mountains ↔ southern Diablo Range puma corridor, shown as a two-tier swath. The darker core band is the lowest-cost 2% of corridor cells (409 km²), a high-confidence corridor; the lighter context band is the lowest-cost 5% (1,021 km²), the permeable flanks around it. The corridor runs through this zone, not along a single line — the band is the honest product at a 1 km grain. The precise US-101 pinch at Coyote Valley is located by road geometry in the crossing map below, not by this swath. No puma locations are shown; this is generalised corridor geometry. Source: least-cost corridor, Decision 33.\n:::\n:::\n\n\n## Where the corridor meets the roads\n\nA corridor is only as good as its worst crossing. Where the swath meets a major\nroad, the road is a barrier whose severity scales with traffic, not just with\npresence — a quiet two-lane road and a freeway are not the same obstacle. So each\ncrossing is ranked by traffic volume.\n\nThe top barrier is **US-101 at Coyote Valley, at 142,000 vehicles a day.** Getting\nthat number right took a correction. An earlier version of the analysis, using a\nspatial traffic-data join, dropped US-101 here to a modelled floor of about 80,000\n— a 43% underestimate — and let a lower-traffic arterial outrank it. The fix\nreferenced the traffic data along the highway route by postmile, recovered the\nreal 142k, and re-ran the corridor (Decision 34). US-101 is now correctly the\ntop-ranked crossing.\n\nOne honest detail in that ranking: crossings are ranked *within* traffic-data\nconfidence tiers, not on the raw number alone. A road whose volume is measured\ndirectly outranks one whose volume is filled in by name-matching, even when the\nfilled-in number is close. This stops a lower-confidence estimate from\nmasquerading as the top barrier — the exact failure the US-101 correction fixed.\n\n## The network, and a distinction that matters\n\nWidening from the single corridor to the whole system of large habitat blocks —\nthe 29 cores of at least 30 km² — gives a connectivity network: each core linked\nto its geometric neighbours, each link routed through the resistance surface. Two\nfindings come out of it, and holding them apart is the point.\n\n**Coyote Valley is the efficient link, and the traffic barrier — but not the\nstructural weak point.** The network routes Santa Cruz to Diablo not as the forced\ndirect line but as a cheap stepping-stone chain through the south-Bay cores, at\nroughly half the cost of the direct route. Those Coyote Valley cores are the\n*efficient* path; losing them forces the animal onto the costlier direct crossing.\nSo Coyote Valley matters twice — as the real, protectable connection, and as the\n142k traffic barrier astride it — but it is a strong link, not a weak one.\n\n**The structural weak links are the cross-bay spans.** The costliest connections\nin the network are the long, high-resistance routes that would join the\nPeninsula / Santa Cruz cluster to the East-Bay / Diablo cluster *across the\ncentral urbanized Bay* — spans of 40 to 60 km with no stepping stones between them.\nThe central Bay effectively splits the system into two subnetworks, joined\nefficiently only at the south end, through Coyote Valley. This matches what is\nalready known: Santa Cruz Mountains pumas are famously isolated, connected to the\nDiablo Range mainly via the south Bay.\n\nThe traffic pinch and the structural weak point are therefore two different things\non two different axes. Coyote Valley is a traffic-mitigation priority — a real\ncorridor with a dangerous road across it. The cross-bay gaps are a fragmentation\nfinding — evidence of what is already disconnected.\n\n**And the cross-bay links are not corridors to build.** This distinction matters\nenough to state plainly: a 60 km least-cost path across the developed\nbayshore is at the edge of biological plausibility. The network method forces a\nroute between geometric neighbours whether or not a real one exists, and the\nrouting dutifully finds the least-bad line across an essentially impassable\nbarrier. Those longest cross-bay links mean *these clusters are effectively\ndisconnected*, not *here is a corridor to protect*. Read them as fragmentation\nevidence. The south-Bay Coyote Valley chain, by contrast, is a real connection\nworth protecting.\n\n## What this section is, and is not\n\nThese corridors are hypotheses about movement drawn from a landscape-cost model,\nnot tracks from collared animals. No puma was followed to draw them; there is no\ntelemetry here to confirm them. Shifting the model's weights within a plausible\nrange moves the corridor by up to about 1.5 km, so the routes are stable in\nlocation but not exact to the metre. They are the best structural estimate of where\nconnectivity runs — a well-founded hypothesis, and the right basis for asking where\nto look and where to protect, but a hypothesis.\n\nEvery puma layer here is shown as generalised geometry — bands and routes, never\npoints. That is deliberate and required: precise puma locations are a poaching and\ndisturbance risk, so the project never publishes them. Nothing in this section is\nfine enough to reverse-engineer a den, a kill site, or a camera. The corridors are\nabout where an animal *could* move, at the scale of the landscape, not about where\nany individual animal *is*.\n",
